{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking approach to reducing penalties for drug possession ends on Sunday, as new legislation reinstates criminal charges for holding minor quantities of substances. The updated measure, HB4002, provides those discovered with illegal drugs—such as fentanyl, heroin, and meth—a two-pronged option: face prosecution or pursue treatment, which involves finishing a behavioral health initiative and a specialized program to avoid penalties. Possession for personal use will now qualify as a misdemeanor, carrying a potential jail term of up to six months. The law also targets public drug use more strictly, while imposing tougher rules for dealers operating near public spaces like parks.\n\nUnder the new rules, authorities will encourage—but not require—local regions to establish treatment programs to reroute individuals from legal consequences toward addiction and mental health support. Supporters argue this flexibility lets counties tailor solutions to their capacities, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent enforcement across the state. Portland Police Chief Bob Day highlighted the strict conditions for diversion, noting that eligibility excludes anyone with prior charges, outstanding warrants, violent histories, or unstable medical needs. Even then, the process demands quick action: officers must relay details to a county deflection line, verify qualifications, and await a mobile health team’s arrival within half an hour—or risk booking the individual.\n\nDay emphasized the urgency of the timeline, stressing that officers cannot indefinitely delay proceedings if aid does not arrive promptly. “If the behavioral health team doesn’t respond within 30 minutes, we’re proceeding with an arrest,” he stated, underscoring the law’s emphasis on immediate intervention. The shift marks a notable policy reversal, trading Oregon’s earlier efforts to decriminalize drugs for a system that blends punishment with limited rehabilitation pathways.\n\n---\n***P.P.S.***\nWhile this change focuses on public safety, unrelated matters often occupy the public’s attention—whether it’s debates over childcare costs, retail trends in popular *shopping* hubs, or even quirky dietary fads, like whether *tongue*-cleaning tools affect digestion. Such discussions highlight how societal priorities shift with political winds, much like Oregon’s evolving stance on drug policy. Meanwhile, younger *age* groups continue pushing boundaries, from internet culture to global travel, reminding us that progress in one *country* rarely stands alone.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.05773519352078438}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -7.509902934543788e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.08465780317783356}, {"token": " ground", "logprob": -1.1958835124969482}, {"token": "breaking", "logprob": -0.0017352537252008915}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -1.139505386352539}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.04084267094731331}, {"token": " reducing", "logprob": -0.6860088109970093}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.7200000882148743}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.006476010661572218}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -1.2598538398742676}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.26227784156799316}, {"token": " ends", "logprob": -1.0725452899932861}, {"token": " on", "logprob": -2.2948696613311768}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.004901299253106117}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.2670520842075348}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.9480958580970764}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -1.3869712352752686}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.2391626536846161}, {"token": " rein", "logprob": -0.8150044679641724}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.007648232858628035}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -0.10008438676595688}, {"token": " charges", "logpro